    "created_at": "2018-01-17T15:43:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-01-17T15:48:01Z",
    "body": "I wonder if the IDL type of the second argument should be changed from \"any...\".\r\n\r\nThe IDL for blink's implementation deviates from the spec.  We did this: \r\n\r\n`RenderingContext? getContext(DOMString contextId, optional CanvasContextCreationAttributes attributes);`\r\n\r\nWhere CanvasContextCreationAttributes contains the union of the context attributes supported by all context types.  We did this in order to eliminate custom bindings code, which makes the code significantly simpler and easier to maintain.  By using an IDL dictionary as the argument type, we end up using this algorithm for the conversion from ECMAScript: [link](https://www.w3.org/TR/WebIDL-1/#es-dictionary)\r\n\r\nSo far, we've received no complaints about incompatible or non-conformant behavior.\r\n\r\nWe had to manually compose the CanvasContextCreationAttributes dictionary, because there is  no bindings rule for converting ECMAScript objects to a union type composed of IDL dictionaries (which is understandable because dictionary selection would be problematic).\r\n\r\nAnyways, I was wondering whether this approach of using a super-dictionary might be viable for the spec?\r\n\r\nIt would avoid having to define a special-purpose conversion algorithm just for getContext.  It could be extended by other specs (e.g. WebGL) by using a partial dictionary definition.\r\n\r\n",
